20 years after the united states ejected  the taliban from power they have  returned to kabul and are in charge of  afghanistan over the spring and summer  the insurgents made quick work sweeping  across the country sometimes seizing as  many as half a dozen cities in one week  the new rulers appear content with  grabbing control and say they do not  have plans for any violent and chaotic  sacking of the city  for now the streets of kabul are  relatively calm and quiet save what  we've seen from the airport the taliban  appears to want an orderly takeover not  a messy transition  that's exactly what afghanistan's  neighbors want too they are watching  very closely with china saying it is  ready for quote friendly relations here  are comments from both pakistan's  foreign minister and from china's  special envoy for afghan affairs  this situation the situation is fluid  it's evolving  in this situation we have to talk and  play our role very responsibly  god willing pakistan's role will be  positive  we have clearly said that we have not  intervened in their internal matters  nor do we have any such intentions  we do not have any favorites in  afghanistan  afghan people have to decide about their  future  whether it's afghan taliban  or afghan rulers  also need to make sure that the chaos  will not spill over into other countries  china in particular has a 90 kilometer  borderline with afghanistan and they are  our neighbours  so we hope that afghanistan can achieve  peace and reconciliation as soon as  possible and stop the chaos and prevent  the threat of terrorism from spilling  over into neighbouring countries peace  tranquility and security must be  maintained  we have andrew small with us to talk  about the regional implications of the  taliban takeover he's senior  transatlantic fellow at the german  marshall fund of the united states  andrew welcome to the program  thanks for having me on  now we have pakistan and china both  clearly nervous about developments they  keep talking about peace  it seems like the american and nato  presence all these years did buy that  kind of security for these two countries  from china's perspective the u.s  security presence was kind of the lesser  of two evils they were concerned about a  large-scale u.s military presence in  their backyard but they saw the benefits  that particularly the kind of slimmed  down version had when it came to  containing instability in the region and  also going after specific terrorist  concerns that that china had in  afghanistan and some of the pakistani  border areas as well they did ultimately  want to see the us withdraw but they  wanted to see the u.s only stepping out  at a point in which a political  settlement had been reached for pakistan  it's it's a more mixed picture i i think  but but they too will be concerned and  are concerned about the immediate  spillover effects of what's happening  even though of course this is the policy  they've backed they've supported the  taliban for some time  pakistan has been the rear operating  base so this this outcome is something  that in a very different way from china  they they really own  now i want to actually uh jump on that  you mentioned pakistan being a safe  haven for the taliban for the last 20  years and critics including a number of  western countries have accused islamabad  of outright supporting the taliban  that's very confusing because they're  worried about the refugees what does  pakistan want here  i mean they are concerned about the  refugees there is a different quality of  relationship with with the taliban now  versus when the pakistan was kind of  first backing their their rise to power  in particular the rise of insurgent  groups like the the pakistani taliban  the ttp in pakistan itself  have changed the balance of concerns  they now the pakistanis also don't want  afghanistan used as a rear operating  base by the pakistani taliban to conduct  attacks on their territory and they have  hosted large number of large numbers of  refugees for many years millions of  afghan refugees they will be concerned  about the considerable numbers that  they're expecting to to flow to their  borders  that's very interesting now let's pivot  to china  you mentioned this earlier about its  fears uh in regards to terrorism it  shares a border with afghanistan  something a lot of people don't know and  of course beijing claims there's an  insurgency within its own borders  with  uyghur separatists and this has given  china the excuse to build hundreds of  camps and intern over a million ethnic  minorities so what is china thinking and  calculating now in regards to that  well  the uyghur question has really been the  standing issue between china and the  taliban going all the way back to the  time that mullah omar and taliban were  were lost in power in  afghanistan at the turn of the  millennium the late 90s the main issue  between them was always the question of  whether there were uyghurs uh what china  then called and still calls etim  being hosted in various ways by by the  taliban on on the territory that they  control if you look at the meeting that  took place between wangi and mula  baradar recently if you look down the  the readout of the meeting it's the same  issues being raised two decades later  it's still about the relationship that  the taliban have with uiga groups which  which again china is still calling etim  but i think it is clear that there have  been turkistan islamic party fighters in  afghanistan so this isn't  as it was i think at certain points in  the past a kind of completely fictitious  immaterial concern just designed as an  excuse to justify what china's doing in  xinjiang there is a real  counter-terrorism concern that china has  in afghanistan at the same time and  there are also wider groups operating  there that have made china target in a  way that they they hadn't even a couple  of decades ago and  what china expects of course of its  neighboring countries if it's the  pakistanis or the central asians is that  uyghurs are killed or handed over or  expelled from the country or  similarly drastic measures that's not  something the taliban have ever been  willing to do  so the demands that china's going to  have in this regard when it comes to a  taliban government and the demands  they've always had of the taliban i  think there's there is this bar that  they're not sure that the taliban are  willing to reach so it is going to be a  question that's that's likely to be a  standing tension between the two sides  got it now  let's take a step back this is not a  great moment for the united states this  is humiliating frankly  and um i want to ask what other  countries in the region including  military allies such as south korea or  the philippines might be thinking about  this or or japan or taiwan take your  pick what is the message they're getting  here  i think you have to separate the policy  itself from the way it was executed i  think  the purpose of withdrawing from  afghanistan and to a certain extent over  a longer arc the drawdown from the  middle east was to refocus on the  indo-pacific on the maritime space on  backing allies and partners in the wider  region i think that seemed to be the  bigger and more important strategic game  so the principle that the us should not  be stuck making vast commitments to land  wars in eurasia and should be refocusing  on the indo-pacific  in some ways that shift that pivot that  rebalancing is something that allies in  the region wanted to see i think the  concern is rather the manner in which  this was was executed whatever you  attribute the the failures to um the  intelligence analysis in terms of the  speed that it took place  what seems to be the poor planning the  whole manner in which it's conducted is  clearly going to be a matter of concern  to uh to to to allies in the asia  pacific um and in the immediate region  the spillover effects uh have direct  repercussions  andrew small thank you so much for  joining us  sure very happy today
 

